    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Bulk open is in: each claim has a checkbox, and an \"Open selected\" button opens the ticked claims together. `bin/e2e` passes all 4 tests in the spec.\n\n**What it does**\n- Selected claims are handled in list order: people in the order they were added, newest claim first within each person. So the numbers they get follow that order.\n- Each claim goes through the same rules as opening it alone. A claim that's already past draft, or dated in a closed period, is left as it is and writes nothing to the audit file. Each claim that opens writes the same audit lines as a single open: `CLAIM_OPENED`, then `ROLLUP`, then `TOTALS`.\n- After the button is pressed, the screen shows how many were opened (`bulk-opened`) and how many were refused (`bulk-refused`), and the selection is cleared.\n\n**Changes**\n- **Server:** a new `POST /api/sheets/{sheetId}/claims/bulk-open` endpoint (`bulk-open.POST.yml`) with four new classes in the `claim` package. To keep one set of rules, it calls the existing single-open steps directly rather than copying their logic: `CheckClaimActionOpen`, `ApplyClaimAction` and `AuditClaimStatus`.\n- **Front end:** in `Claims.tsx`, a `claim-select` checkbox on each claim row and a `BulkOpen` component (the `bulk-open` button and the counts), placed in `People.tsx`. The selection is kept in the URL under `selectedClaims`, the way the app keeps other selection state.\n\n**Choices the spec didn't cover**\n- Selecting an id that isn't on the sheet counts it as refused.\n- Pressing the button with nothing selected is refused, and the error shows under a new `bulk-error` testid.\n- The database schema didn't need to change.",
